From 53a42d7fffa7ed33df5d1f58f1cbf931af49dba0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 18:01:43 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 103/106] decode-link: options of sing-box by their paths, and
 what a link may not set

A link names an option that no link has a name for by its path in the configuration,
after "sb.": sb.tls.fragment=1, sb.multiplex.protocol=h2mux,
sb.transport.headers.User-Agent=... The value is read by the type the option has in
this very sing-box (a flag as 1 or 0, a list by commas, JSON when it begins with a
bracket), so an option of another line is "no such option" here. A path wins over
what the link says otherwise, with a warning; a group of options that has a switch and
is not switched on gets one too. The outbound is built afterwards like any other:
what it refuses is the error of the command.

The names a few clients already use are read as the options they stand for: tfo,
tcp-fast-open, tcp_fast_open; uot, udp-over-tcp; of Throne, tls_fragment,
tls_record_fragment, tls_fragment_fallback_delay, tls_spoof, tls_spoof_method,
mux_protocol, mux_max_connections, mux_min_streams, mux_max_streams, mux_padding,
brutal_up_mbps, brutal_down_mbps. Not the bare mux and fragment: clients mean
different things by each.

A link is somebody else's text: it may tune how its own server is reached and nothing
more. Refused by name or by declaration: references to other objects (detour,
domain_resolver, netns), the local route (bind_interface, the bind addresses,
routing_mark, the network strategy), local files (every *_path), features of the
system (the TLS engine, kTLS), the Hysteria realm, deprecated options, and what the
link itself says (type, tag, server, server_port).

A test walks the options of every outbound type a link makes and holds the result to
a list kept per line of sing-box (testdata/options-1.13.txt, options-1.14.txt): each
option is for a link to set or denied with its reason, and one that a rebase brings
fails the test until somebody has looked at it.
